From 6cc89da6a0e975146d05a22b4c966336ffde9722 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 20:47:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Requeue a mid-turn unreachable-owner persistence
 failure without charging the card

Incident: example-tools/t_01a197166756bd53 run 207 on hermes-hub, 2026-11-13 20:06Z. The bound worker
died with `WorkerPersistenceError: owner_unavailable` and was booked `crashed` (failure 1 of 2). The
same card had survived every earlier planned gateway restart through the restart checkpoint.

Timeline (hermes-sync.log, errors.log, gateway journal, shutdown-watchdog log, task_events):
- 20:01:19 the instant core-update drainer starts. At 20:02:29 `hermes update` asks gateway 4111315 to drain.
- 20:03:11 the worker sends `transcript.append`. Gateway loops stall: 33.5 s at 20:03:29, 29.5 s at
  20:04:38 (GIL pressure). The 20:04:38 log line reaches the journal at 20:07:23.
- 20:04:28 the update gives up ("hermes-gateway still draining after 70s"). The gateway is neither
  restarted nor recovered. Sync passes report it as "active-work unknown" until 20:14.
- 20:04:41 the worker logs a receipt timeout (send 1/2), drops the link and redials. Discovery gets no
  control answer within the 90 s deadline. At 20:06:11 it raises `owner_unavailable`, the turn's
  finalize fails, and the worker exits 1.
- 20:12:35 the loop-liveness watchdog fires. 20:13:14 SIGKILL, 20:13:33 gateway 21016 starts.
- 20:15:30 the observer books run 207 `crashed` from the bound receipt and counts a failure.

Five whys:
1. Why was a failure counted? The sweep saw the bound worker's exit-1 receipt
   `Worker failed: WorkerPersistenceError: owner_unavailable` and booked it `crashed`.
2. Why did that receipt count when `storage_unavailable` does not? `kanban_owner_storage_refusal`
   (a9347a785aa) matched only the storage envelope. An owner the worker cannot reach at all is the same
   owner-side fault, but nothing classified it.
3. Why did the worker get `owner_unavailable` and not a checkpoint? The checkpoint is owner-driven: the
   owner announces `core_update_pause` and snapshots the turn. This owner was wedged, not restarting.
   It never announced anything, and its control socket answered nothing for over 180 s, so the worker's
   bounded resend (2 x 90 s) ran out.
4. Why was the owner wedged? The instant drain asked it to drain under heavy CPU and GIL pressure. The
   update timed out after 70 s and left the gateway running in that state until the liveness watchdog
   killed it 8 minutes later.
5. Why was it not caught? The storage-refusal fix tested only the storage wire code. No test had a
   worker whose owner is alive but silent, which is the only case where the checkpoint path cannot run.

Decision: a worker cannot checkpoint while its owner is unresponsive, because only the owner can take
the checkpoint. The right outcome is the neutral requeue already used for owner refusals. The work tree
survives, and the next run continues from it. The NEUTRAL_STREAK_LIMIT breaker still stops an owner
that never comes back.

Change: `kanban_owner_storage_refusal.proven` accepts exactly two terminal envelopes on the newest
attributable bound receipt (exit 1, whole line): `storage_unavailable` maps to
owner_storage_unavailable as before, and `owner_unavailable` maps to the new `owner_unreachable` kind.
The host-refusal notice and run error describe it truthfully as "turn interrupted".

Proof: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_owner_unreachable_midrun.py replays run 207's two-receipt shape. It
was RED before the change (3 failed: consecutive_failures 1, outcome crashed) and is GREEN after. The 6
related files pass (109 tests), including the owner-restart checkpoint tests and storage-refusal
tests, which are unchanged. `scripts/check --base origin/main` is ok.

Not tested: a live hub replay (needs the merged build installed); the stalled-gateway drain itself
(the update's abort path is out of scope here); the outer client's rc=76 that never reached the board
as a worker_result.
